WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.029
A payment authorization leaves the payment service as bytes.

2
00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:11.503
As JSON text it takes 116 bytes, and any language can read it.

3
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.791
Count what those bytes hold.

4
00:00:13.791 --> 00:00:20.887
Field names, quotes and colons take 55 of them, and the ID is written out as 19 decimal digits.

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.028
A binary encoding with a schema works differently.

6
00:00:24.028 --> 00:00:28.588
Writer and reader share a schema that gives each field a number, one to five.

7
00:00:28.588 --> 00:00:31.135
The bytes carry the number, never the name.

8
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:38.509
Whole numbers are varints: seven bits per byte, and only as many bytes as the value needs.

9
00:00:38.509 --> 00:00:41.873
4,000 fits in 2, and the 19-digit ID in 9.

10
00:00:42.067 --> 00:00:47.321
With every field counted, the record is 42 bytes, against 116.

11
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:51.364
Now a support dashboard in TypeScript reads the JSON.

12
00:00:51.364 --> 00:00:56.784
JSON.parse turns every number into a double, which holds 53 bits of digits.

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.675
Every whole number up to 2 to the 53, about 9 quadrillion, fits exactly.

14
00:01:02.675 --> 00:01:09.216
Our ID is about 200 times bigger, and up there, neighbouring doubles are 256 apart.

15
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.292
So the parser rounds to the nearest one it can hold.

16
00:01:13.292 --> 00:01:19.056
The ID now ends in 4000 instead of 4017, and there's no error and no warning.

17
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:23.433
A refund for that ID finds nothing, or someone else's payment.

18
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:30.811
A reader with the schema knows field 1 is a 64-bit integer, and decodes all 9 bytes exactly.

19
00:01:30.811 --> 00:01:33.451
In JSON, send such IDs as strings.

20
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:35.292
That's what a schema buys.

21
00:01:35.292 --> 00:01:42.116
Numbers replace names, so the bytes shrink, and every field has a declared type, so no reader has to guess.

22
00:01:42.333 --> 00:01:46.111
Text carries every name and leaves the types to each reader.

23
00:01:46.111 --> 00:01:50.581
A schema moves both into an agreement, and the bytes carry only values.
